+A load session owned the application it started, so only closing that session
+unloaded it. Losing the fd any other way -- a crash, a kill, a process that
+simply exits -- left the application resident in TZ, where it cannot be loaded
+again and nothing short of a reboot clears it. Ownership also forced a process
+to stay alive holding an fd for no reason other than to keep the application
+loaded, which is the wrong shape for a daemon that may be socket-activated or
+idle out.
+
+amdtee already answers this: it has no owner and no explicit unload. A TA is
+reference-counted by handle, every session holds a reference, and the last one
+out unloads it (handle_unload_ta() returns -EBUSY while the count is non-zero).
+Do the same here. The registry entry gains a user count, resolving an
+application by name takes a reference, and every path that destroys a session
+gives one back -- including the teardown that runs when a context dies without
+closing anything, which is what makes this crash-safe rather than merely tidy.
+
+Applications the boot chain loaded keep generation 0 and are never unloaded;
+this driver does not own them.
+
+Found by running it: a harness that went away without closing its session left
+the application resident, and the next load failed with -EINVAL from TZ.
+
